        "Claudio Pellegrini",
        "Jörg Rossbach",
        "Tetsuya Ishikawa"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "SASE X-ray free-electron lasers opened femtosecond, atomic-scale observation of matter and have become indispensable instruments across physics, chemistry, and biology.",
        "attribution": "The credited scientists represent essential conceptual, accelerator-development, and operational realization contributions.",
        "maturity": "Multiple facilities have delivered reproducible scientific capability over many years.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Attribution across the extensive international accelerator and facility collaborations is intrinsically difficult."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B012",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Gregory N. Gol'tsman",
        "Roman Sobolewski",
        "Sae Woo Nam"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Superconducting nanowire detectors made highly efficient, low-noise single-photon detection practical and enabled much modern quantum optics and communication.",
        "attribution": "Gol'tsman led the original practical detector development; Sobolewski and Nam made major foundational and performance contributions.",
        "maturity": "The devices are mature, commercialized, and central to demanding photon-counting experiments.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The relative allocation of early conceptual and subsequent device-development credit requires careful historical review."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B015",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Christian J. Bordé",
        "David E. Pritchard",
        "Mark A. Kasevich"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Atom interferometry has provided a powerful quantum-measurement platform for inertial sensing, gravimetry, tests of gravity, and precision metrology.",
        "attribution": "Bordé developed central light-pulse interferometric ideas, while Pritchard and Kasevich made decisive experimental advances.",
        "maturity": "The methods are thoroughly tested and now support both frontier experiments and robust instruments.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The field has several partially overlapping foundational lineages, complicating a three-person allocation."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B007",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Carlton M. Caves",
        "Roman Schnabel"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Squeezed light converted a quantum-optical insight into a demonstrated route beyond shot-noise limits, notably in gravitational-wave interferometry.",
        "attribution": "Caves supplied the decisive quantum-measurement framework and Schnabel made landmark experimental squeezed-light and interferometric contributions.",
        "maturity": "Quantum enhancement has been repeatedly demonstrated in major operating observatories and precision experiments.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The appropriate attribution to broader interferometer-development teams needs close assessment."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
